= Solution
{parent=3H}

See <Coding theory>.

In <Rabin cryptosystem>, choose Blum primes $p,q$, publish $N=pq$, encrypt $m$ as $c=m^2\bmod N$, and decrypt by taking square roots modulo $p$ and $q$ and combining them with CRT; redundancy identifies the intended one of four roots. Here $2355^2\equiv25\pmod{2773}$, while the obvious root is $5$. Thus
$$\gcd(2355-5,2773)=47,\qquad\gcd(2355+5,2773)=59.$$
So $2773=47\cdot59$ is factored, allowing all future square roots and hence all ciphertexts to be decrypted.

